# Golden screen frames are compared byte-for-byte against rendered output,
# which always uses LF. Without this, a Windows checkout converts them to CRLF
# and every golden test fails there while passing everywhere else.
*.golden -text

# The BIP-39 wordlist is a frozen display contract (§6.1.4.2); keep it byte
# stable across platforms.
internal/identity/wordlist.go -text

# Compression dictionaries are binary wire-format artifacts (§7.4). Marking them
# keeps them out of diffs and stops any newline handling touching them.
*.zdict binary

# The dictionary training corpus is an INPUT to a committed artifact, so its
# bytes have to be the same everywhere. dict1.zdict was trained on these files
# with LF endings and the ratio floors were measured against them; a CRLF
# checkout would quietly measure a different corpus than the one the dictionary
# was built from. It also broke the post separator outright on Windows, which is
# how this was found.
internal/dictcorpus/data/** -text

# The conformance corpus is a frozen wire-format contract (§12.6), and the whole
# point of it is that the bytes do not move. JSON parsing would survive a CRLF
# checkout, but "immutable except on Windows" is not the property being claimed —
# and a third-party implementation is meant to be able to hash these files.
internal/conformance/testdata/** -text

# SQL migrations are applied verbatim and hashed in schema_migrations.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Meshtastic protobuf bindings are generated by scripts/genproto.sh from the
# pinned submodule and committed so that building needs neither protoc nor the
# submodule. Marking them keeps them out of diffs and language statistics.
internal/meshtastic/meshpb/*.pb.go linguist-generated=true
